+# Self-Update Summary
+
+## Original Requirements
+
+The user requested a self-update mechanism for cpe with several key requirements:
+
+### Explicit commands
+- `cpe update` — resolve latest GitHub release, compare against baked-in version, download matching asset, verify SHA256 checksum, and atomically replace the running binary. Report old vs new versions.
+- `cpe update --check` — report current vs latest version without downloading or installing anything.
+
+### Automatic updating
+- **TUI (`cpe start`)**: check every 6 hours in background; auto-install when a newer release exists; show persistent yellow footer badge: `↑ vX.Y.Z installed — restart to apply`.
+- **Short-lived CLI commands**: throttled check (max once per 24h, cached). If update available, print one stderr line nudge: `update available: vX.Y.Z — run cpe update`. Short-lived commands never install automatically.
+
+### Configuration and guards
+- `update_auto?: boolean` in `AppConfig`: default `true`. Setting to `false` disables both TUI auto-loop and CLI nudges (but `cpe update` still works).
+- Dev builds (`CPE_VERSION === 'dev'`) never check, nudge, or auto-install.
+
+### Release integrity
+- Verify binaries against a `SHA256SUMS` file published with every release. Fail closed on mismatch.
+
+## What Was Built
+
+The following phases were implemented:
+
+- **Phase 01 — Release checksums (SHA256SUMS)**: Updated `.github/workflows/release.yml` to generate and include a `SHA256SUMS` file for all binaries in every release. Updated `scripts/install.sh` to verify these checksums when available, with fallback support for older releases.
+- **Phase 02 — Updater core module (`src/update/`)**: Implemented the underlying engine for version resolution (parsing GitHub redirect headers), numerical comparison of versions (handling prereleases correctly), state persistence in `~/.local/state/cpe/update-check.json`, and verified atomic binary installation using filesystem rename operations.
+- **Phase 03 — CLI surface: `cpe update`, config flag, nudge**: Exposed the updater engine via a new `cpe update` command (supporting `--check`) and added the `update_auto` configuration flag. Implemented a throttled post-command "nudge" for short-lived commands that alerts users of available updates without interrupting their workflow.
+- **Phase 04 — TUI auto-update loop + footer badge**: Implemented a background update loop for long-running TUI sessions (every 6 hours) and an in-memory store to manage the "restart required" state. Added persistent yellow badges to all three major TUI modes (`Watch`, `Manage`, `Bench`) that appear after a successful auto-update but before a restart.
+
+## Lessons Learned
+
+- **Atomic Swaps**: Using `rename()` for binary replacement is essential on Linux/macOS because overwriting an active executable will fail with `ETXTBSY`. The running process continues to use the old inode, making it safe while allowing subsequent launches to pick up the new binary.
+- **Redirect Parsing vs API**: Avoiding the GitHub API by parsing redirect headers for `/releases/latest` is a much cheaper and more reliable way to fetch version information without hitting rate limits or requiring authentication.
+- **Nudge Throttling**: A 24h cache is necessary for CLI nudges to prevent spamming users with update notifications on every single command execution.
+- **Badge Persistence**: Because the TUI re-renders around interactive subprocesses, using a module-level store (rather than local component state) was required to ensure the "restart to apply" badge persists across these transitions.
